data.reasoningText:
OK so `ManagedServiceIdentityType` is a readonly struct with two custom JsonConverters. It does NOT implement IJsonModel. The converters are used to serialize/deserialize the type field.

Looking at the usage in ManagedServiceIdentity.Serialization.cs:
```csharp
// line 31: Serialize - writes the "type" property with value
JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, ManagedServiceIdentityType, jOptions);
// line 206: Deserialize - reads the "type" property  
type = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<ManagedServiceIdentityType>($"{{{property}}}");


```

The converter's Write method outputs `"type": "value"` and Read expects `{"type": "value"}`. For AOT compatibility, I need to replace the `JsonSerializer.Serialize/Deserialize` calls with direct converter logic—writing the property name and string value directly instead of going through the serializer, though I need to account for the V3 converter path as well.

Looking at how the Write method chains through the converter, when called via `JsonSerializer`, the `jOptions` parameter contains the `JsonSerializerOptions` that might include the `ManagedServiceIdentityTypeV3Converter`.

```

csharp
public override void Write(Utf8JsonWriter writer, ManagedServiceIdentity model, JsonSerializerOptions options)
{
    model.Write(writer, new ModelReaderWriterOptions("W"), options);
}
```

So `jOptions` gets passed to `JsonSerializer.Serialize` calls for both the `ManagedServiceIdentityType` and dictionary values, meaning any custom converters in the options would be used. For AOT compatibility, I need to inline the serialization logic instead of relying on the converter, since the `ManagedServiceIdentityType` converter just writes a simple key-value pair with special handling for the `SystemAssignedUserAssigned` case in V3.

