This file has 4 warnings from `JsonSerializer.Serialize/Deserialize` calls:

### Line 31: Serialize ManagedServiceIdentityType
Current code:
```csharp
            JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, ManagedServiceIdentityType, jOptions);
```

The `ManagedServiceIdentityType` is a readonly struct with a custom `[JsonConverter]` attribute. The converter's Write method (in ManagedServiceIdentityType.cs) does:
```csharp
writer.WritePropertyName("type");
writer.WriteStringValue(model.ToString());
```

Replace line 31 with inline logic that avoids JsonSerializer:
```csharp
            writer.WritePropertyName("type"u8);
            writer.WriteStringValue(ManagedServiceIdentityType.ToString());
```

### Line 49: Serialize UserAssignedIdentity
Current code:
```csharp
                    JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, item.Value, jOptions);
```

`UserAssignedIdentity` implements `IJsonModel<UserAssignedIdentity>` and `IUtf8JsonSerializable`. Replace with:
```csharp
                    writer.WriteObjectValue(item.Value, options);
```

Note: `options` is the `ModelReaderWriterOptions` parameter of the method.

### Line 206: Deserialize ManagedServiceIdentityType
Current code:
```csharp
                    type = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<ManagedServiceIdentityType>($"{{{property}}}");
```

This wraps the property into JSON like `{"type":"SystemAssigned"}` then deserializes. The converter reads the "type" property value. Inline the logic:
```csharp
                    if (property.Value.ValueKind == JsonValueKind.Null)
                    {
